{"id":"d977d470-29c5-4c75-83f8-773dcb35134d","object":"chat.completion","created":1785005375,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children_map = {}\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        parent = n['parent']\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            children_map.setdefault(parent, []).append(n['id'])\\n    # compute subtree required flags\\n    subtree_required = {}\\n    def dfs(node_id):\\n        node = node_map[node_id]\\n        req = node['symbol'] in required_symbols\\n        for child in children_map.get(node_id, []):\\n            if dfs(child):\\n                req = True\\n        subtree_required[node_id] = req\\n        return req\\n    # root is always present\\n    dfs('root')\\n\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n\\n    while True:\\n        candidates = []\\n        for nid in retained:\\n            node = node_map[nid]\\n            if not node['removable']:\\n                continue\\n            if node['parent'] is None:\\n                continue  # cannot remove root\\n            if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n                continue\\n            # must have no retained children\\n            children = children_map.get(nid, [])\\n            if any(c in retained for c in children):\\n                continue\\n            # subtree must contain no required symbol\\n            if subtree_required[nid]:\\n                continue\\n            candidates.append(node)\\n        if not candidates:\\n            break\\n        # deepest first, then by id ascending (string compare)\\n        candidates.sort(key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n        selected = candidates[0]\\n        removed_order.append(selected['id'])\\n        retained.remove(selected['id'])\\n\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': removed_order,\\n        'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) according to the problem description. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" for SE-PE-01. The case payload contains nodes (a list of node objects) and required_symbols (list of strings). Each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. The goal: reduce the tree by removing removable nodes, preserving required symbols, and following rules: deeper nodes before shallower, never remove root, required symbols, or nodes with retained children. Tentatively remove only if all required symbols remain represented. Use node ID as tiebreak at equal depth. Return removed_node_ids (order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted).\n\nThe algorithm: We need to simulate a reduction process where we iterate through nodes in a certain order (deeper first, then by id for same depth) and attempt to remove each removable node that is not root and not a required symbol, and check if its removal would still keep all required symbols present (including symbols of its descendants? Actually the required_symbols are symbols that must remain somewhere in the tree. If we remove a node, its subtree is gone. So we need to ensure that for each required symbol, there is at least one node with that symbol that is not removed. Also we cannot remove a node that has children that are retained (i.e., if any child is not removed, then we can't remove the node? The description says \"never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So we only remove leaf nodes or nodes whose entire subtree is being removed? Actually typical tree reduction: you can remove a node and its entire subtree if doing so preserves required symbols. But the rule \"a node with retained children\" means if any child is kept, you cannot remove that parent. So essentially we can only remove a node if all its descendants are also removed (i.e., it's a leaf in the set of retained nodes? Hmm). Alternatively, we could remove a node and its subtree. But then the children become orphans. Usually in tree reduction, you can delete a node and reconnect its children to its parent, but the description says \"deletion and replacement candidates\" but also \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" and \"Perses syntax-guided reduction\". Perses algorithm: remove a node and replace its subtree with a placeholder? In this task, the output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. There is no replacement. So likely we are only allowed to remove entire subtrees (nodes and all descendants). The condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" means if any descendant is not removed, the node cannot be removed. So we only remove subtrees that are entirely removed. That is consistent with typical tree pruning: we remove a node only if all its descendants are also removed (i.e., it's the root of a removed subtree). So we need to decide which removable nodes to remove, in order, such that after removal, the remaining nodes (retained) satisfy required symbols.\n\nThe algorithm: Start with all nodes retained. Then we attempt to remove nodes in order: deeper first (higher depth), then by id for tie. For each node, if it is removable, not root, not a required symbol (by id? Actually \"required symbol\" means the node's symbol is in required_symbols? The description says \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So we cannot remove a node whose symbol is in required_symbols. Also, if we remove this node, we must check that all required symbols still appear in the retained nodes (including nodes not removed). Additionally, since we are removing subtrees, we must ensure that no descendant is already removed? Actually we will process in order: when we encounter a node, its descendants (deeper) have already been considered. If a descendant was removed, then this node's subtree is already partially removed. But we cannot remove a node if any of its children are retained. Since we process deeper first, if a descendant was kept, then this node cannot be removed later because it would have retained children. So the algorithm: iterate nodes in descending depth, then ascending id. For each node, if it is removable, not root, symbol not in required_symbols, and all its children are either not present (shouldn't happen) or all children are also removed (i.e., their ids are not in retained set, but we haven't removed this node yet), then we can consider removing it. But we need to check that after removing it, all required symbols still exist in the retained set. However, if a descendant is a required symbol, and we remove this node, that descendant would be removed as well (since entire subtree). So we cannot remove a node that is an ancestor of any required symbol node. So we need to know which nodes are ancestors of required symbols. Simpler: before removal, compute for each node whether its subtree contains any required symbol. If yes, then cannot remove. Also root cannot be removed. And if node's symbol is required, cannot remove. And if any child is retained (not removed), cannot remove. Since we process deeper first, we can maintain a set of removed nodes and a set of retained nodes. Initially all nodes retained. For each node in order, if it is currently retained, and removable, and not root, and its symbol not in required_symbols, and its subtree contains no required symbol (i.e., none of its descendants (including itself) have a symbol in required_symbols), then we can remove it. But careful: descendant nodes may have already been removed. However, if any descendant is a required symbol, it must be retained (since we cannot remove required symbol nodes). So that descendant will still be retained, meaning the node has a retained child/descendant, and thus cannot be removed. So the condition \"no required symbol in subtree\" is equivalent to checking that no descendant (including itself) has a required symbol, and since required symbol nodes cannot be removed, they are retained. So we can check if any node in the subtree (including itself) has symbol in required_symbols and is currently retained. But if some required symbol node is in that subtree, then the node cannot be removed because removing it would eliminate that required symbol. So we can compute for each node a boolean \"has_required\" initially, and update as removals happen? Actually required symbols nodes are always retained, so their ancestors cannot be removed. So we can precompute for each node the set of required symbols in its subtree. Then we can only remove a node if that set is empty and its symbol is not required.\n\nAdditionally, we need to ensure that after removing a node, we don't break any remaining required symbols? The precomputation takes care of that because if a node's subtree has no required, then removing it won't remove any required symbol. However, careful: required symbols might be present in other parts of the tree (e.g., sibling subtrees) - that's fine. So condition: node is removable, not root, symbol not required, no required symbol in its entire subtree (including itself). Then we can remove it. And also we must check that its children are all already removed? Actually if we remove a node, we remove its entire subtree, including all descendants regardless of whether they are removable or not (except root? But root is only one). Since we process deeper first, we may have already removed some descendants? But if we decide to remove the node, we also need to remove all its descendants that are not already removed. However, if any descendant is required (symbol), we cannot remove the node. So all descendants are either removable or non-removable but not required. Non-removable nodes that are not required can be removed as part of the subtree? The rule \"never remove a required symbol\" prevents that, but non-removable nodes that are not required? The description says \"never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" It doesn't say you cannot remove a non-removable node. However, the \"removable\" attribute is part of the node data; nodes with removable=false are presumably not candidates for removal. But if we remove a parent, we would also remove its non-removable children. Is that allowed? The problem says \"Tentatively remove a node only if all required symbols remain represented.\" It doesn't say non-removable nodes can't be removed as part of subtree. But the input marks some nodes as removable=true/false. It would be odd to remove a non-removable node indirectly. Looking at Perses: removable nodes are those that can be deleted individually; non-removable nodes are structural. Typically, in grammar-guided reduction, you can only delete nodes that are marked as deletable (removable). So if a node has removable=false, you cannot delete it, even as part of removing its parent? Possibly the parent removal would force removal of the child, but that would violate the child's removability. So likely we can only remove nodes that are removable, one by one, and only if the node has no retained children. That implies we only remove leaf nodes (in the current retained tree) that are removable. Because if a node has children that are retained, you cannot remove it. So the process is iterative: we repeatedly find removable leaf nodes (in the current tree) that are not root, not required symbol, and whose removal doesn't remove any required symbol. And we remove them one by one, updating the tree.\n\nBut the description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we need to order candidates by depth descending, then id ascending. At each step, we consider the deepest removable leaf that satisfies conditions. But note: after removing a node, the tree changes: its parent may become a leaf. So we need to recalculate which nodes are leaves (no retained children). That suggests an algorithm:\n\n1. Build a mapping from parent_id to children. Also map id->node.\n2. Initially, retained set = all node ids.\n3. For each node, compute its \"has_required\" flag: whether any node in its subtree (including itself) has a symbol in required_symbols. (We can compute via DFS from leaves up.)\n4. Iteratively, find all candidate nodes that are:\n   - retained\n   - removable == True\n   - symbol not in required_symbols\n   - parent is not null (not root)\n   - no retained children (i.e., all children are either not in retained or removed)\n   - has_required == False (i.e., removing this node does not eliminate any required symbol)\n5. Among those candidates, select the one with deepest depth (largest depth), and for equal depth, smallest id (lexicographic? numeric? ids are strings like \"n1\", \"n2\". Use string comparison? The spec says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" Likely lexical order since IDs are strings. But we can sort by depth descending, then id ascending (string compare).)\n6. Remove that node: remove its id from retained set. Also, this node's subtree is removed; since we only remove leaf nodes, its children are already removed (because if they were retained, it wouldn't be a leaf). So just remove the node itself.\n7. After removal, update the candidate set (some nodes may become leaves, e.g., parent of removed node). Also update has_required? Actually has_required is static because required symbols cannot be removed, so the presence of required symbols in subtrees doesn't change. But careful: if a node's subtree had no required, after removing it, nothing changes. So we can keep static has_required.\n8. Repeat until no more candidates.\n9. The removed_node_ids list should be in order of removal. The retained_node_ids should be sorted (presumably by id? The spec says \"sorted retained IDs\" - lexicographic order). So output sorted list.\n\nCheck test fixtures: Each case has nodes and required_symbols [\"root\",\"s1\"]. Root is root depth0, not removable. Nodes: n1 (s1) depth1, removable false; n2 (s2) depth1 removable true; n3 (s0) depth1 removable false; n4 (s1) depth2 removable true; n5 (s2) depth2 removable false; n6 (s0) depth2 removable true. Required symbols: root and s1. The root is always retained. s1 appears at n1 (non-removable) and n4 (removable). So we must keep at least one s1. Since n1 is non-removable, we cannot remove it. n4 is removable but has s1. If we remove n4, we would lose that s1, but n1 still has s1, so it's okay. So we can potentially remove n4 if it's a leaf? n4's parent is n2. n2 has children: n4. n2 is removable. n4 is depth2 leaf. n6 depth2 parent n1. n5 depth2 parent n3. So candidate leaves: n4 (removable, s1? symbol s1 is required, so cannot remove because its symbol is in required? Actually we must never remove a required symbol node. So n4's symbol is s1 which is in required_symbols, so cannot remove n4 even though it's removable. So n4 is not a candidate. Similarly, n6 symbol s0 not required, removable, leaf. n6's parent is n1 which is non-removable. n6's subtree has no required symbol (only s0), so candidate. Also n2 is depth1, removable, but has child n4 which is retained (since n4 cannot be removed because required symbol). So n2 has retained child, cannot remove. n3 non-removable. So only candidate is n6? Also n2 if n4 were removed? But n4 cannot be removed. So only n6. Then after removing n6, its parent n1 becomes leaf? n1 has other children? No, only n6. n1 is non-removable, so not a candidate. Then no more candidates. So removed order: [\"n6\"]. Retained: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. Sorted: [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually sort strings: \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\". That matches? Let's see if that is the expected behavior. The artifact description mentions \"grammar-valid deletion and replacement candidates\" but we have no replacement. Perhaps there is more nuance: The task is from Perses: they have \"grammar-guided node reduction\" where you can delete nodes and replace with other nodes? But here output only shows removed and retained. So likely it's just removal of removable leaf nodes that preserve required symbols.\n\nAnother possibility: they might allow removing a node even if it has retained children, but that would be replaced by its children (i.e., the node is removed and its children become children of its parent). That's a common operation in Perses: \"delete a node and splice its children into its parent\". That would be a \"replacement\" candidate. The artifact mentions \"deletion and replacement candidates\". So maybe we are allowed to delete a node and replace it with its children (i.e., remove the node but keep the subtree). That would change the tree structure. However, the output only has removed_node_ids and retained_node_ids. If we replace a node with its children, the node itself is removed, but its children are retained. That would mean a node can be removed even if it has children, as long as the children are retained. But then the condition \"never remove a node with retained children\" would be violated if we interpret \"retained children\" as children that are still in the tree after removal. In the replacement scenario, the children are still retained, so that would be removing a node with retained children, which is forbidden. So maybe the interpretation is that we only remove leaf nodes. That matches the explicit statement.\n\nAlternatively, maybe \"retained children\" means children that are not removed in the same operation. If we remove a node and replace it with its children, the children are not removed; but the node is removed, which would be a node with retained children. So the rule explicitly forbids that. So only leaf removal.\n\nGiven the test fixtures, the only removable leaf that satisfies not being required and not having required in subtree is n6. So output would be as above. But we need to verify with other cases. The second case has similar structure. So likely that's the intended algorithm.\n\nBut also note the artifact: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" That implies we should order candidates by depth, but after removal, new leaves may appear. To handle ties at same depth, use node ID. So we need to implement iterative selection.\n\nPotential edge: What if there are multiple nodes at same depth and we need to choose one? Pick smallest ID.\n\nImplementation details:\n\nGiven case payload: case is a dict with \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". nodes list, each node has id (string), parent (string or null), symbol, depth (int), removable (bool), utility (float, not used). We'll build map id->node, and parent->children list.\n\nCompute subtree required flags: do post-order traversal. Since tree is rooted at \"root\", we can do DFS from root. For each node, compute has_required = (node.symbol in required_symbols) OR any child has_required. Store in dict.\n\nMain loop: retained = set(all_ids). removed_order = [].\n\nWhile True:\n  candidates = []\n  for node_id in retained:\n    node = nodes_map[node_id]\n    if node.removable and node.parent is not None and node.symbol not in required_symbols:\n      # check if any child of this node is retained\n      children = children_map.get(node_id, [])\n      has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained for child_id in children)\n      if not has_retained_child:\n        # check subtree has no required symbol\n        if not subtree_has_required[node_id]: # this includes node itself, so if node.symbol not required, good.\n          candidates.append(node)\n  if not candidates:\n    break\n  # sort by depth descending, then id ascending (string compare)\n  candidates.sort(key=lambda n: (-n.depth, n.id))\n  selected = candidates[0]\n  # remove selected\n  removed_order.append(selected.id)\n  retained.remove(selected.id)\n  # no need to update subtree_has_required? No because required symbols can't be removed, so static.\n  # continue\n\nAfter loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(list(retained)) (lexicographic order). Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_order, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nCheck root: root.parent is None, so skipped.\n\nWhat about nodes that are non-removable but are leaf? They stay.\n\nPotential issue: if a required symbol node is removable (like n4), we must not remove it. Our check node.symbol not in required_symbols handles that. But also subtree_has_required includes required symbols in descendants. If a node's descendant (like a child) has required symbol, that child is retained (since required), so the node will have retained child, so not a candidate anyway. So the checking is redundant but safe.\n\nNow, ensure that we process deeper nodes first. In the iterative loop, we always select deepest candidate. That mimics the description \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" However, we need to ensure we don't remove a node that would later allow removal of a deeper node? Actually deeper nodes are already considered first. Since we remove leaves, after removal, new leaves appear at the same or higher depth? Actually the parent becomes leaf if all its children are removed. Parent depth is less than child depth. So by removing deepest first, we allow removal of shallower nodes later. That's correct.\n\nBut is it guaranteed that the deepest node that is a candidate is the one we should remove first? The description \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes\" suggests ordering the evaluation, but not necessarily iterative? It might mean when sorting candidates, depth is primary key. That's what we did. But it also says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" That implies we cannot remove a node if any descendant is retained. So the deepest nodes are leaves. So we must process leaves first. That's consistent.\n\nNow, about \"registered_candidate_order\": Might be something else, but we ignore for now.\n\nOne more nuance: The artifact says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: grammar_valid, interestingness_preserved, registered_candidate_order, stable_ties.\" So we need to ensure grammar_valid (maybe respecting the grammar? Not needed), interestingness_preserved (ensuring required symbols remain), registered_candidate_order (maybe order of removal is according to depth then id), stable_ties (use id). So our approach satisfies that.\n\nNow implement solve(case). Use only standard library.\n\nWrite the Python function.\n\nReturn JSON-serializable object directly (dict). Ensure no mutation of case.\n\nTesting with first fixture: Expected output? Not given, but we can infer. We'll output as described.\n\nPotential complication: What if multiple nodes have same depth and same id? Not possible. So fine.\n\nNow produce the implementation as a string for the JSON envelope.\n\nWe'll write the code, then create JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value as the escaped source.\n\nMake sure no extra whitespace in the JSON output. Use json.dumps to escape.\n\nThe entire response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". No other keys.\n\nNow produce."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2169,"completion_tokens":5064,"total_tokens":7233,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4596},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":121},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}